notes[2]:
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T07:17:58.609Z"
    author: "harness:opencode"
    text: "Composition map for the RL/eval entitlement (recorded 2026-07-27, from the full-ecosystem review pass pm-89qv6b): the story body names four missing pieces and each already has an owning item, so this story is the composition layer, not four new features.\n\n1. Verdict computability -> pm-e96opw (executable acceptance criteria). The grader cannot parse prose AC; once every criterion carries a machine-checkable predicate with a recorded result, the episode verdict is pm close --validate-close semantics run over recorded state, with zero environment-specific grader code. Edge added: blocked_by pm-e96opw.\n\n2. Seed reproducibility -> pm-rbcvt2 (reproducible workspace instances). Byte-identical episodes need seeded identifiers, an injectable clock, and deterministic ordering — exactly that item's scope. Edge added: blocked_by pm-rbcvt2.\n\n3. Episode boundary (agent-visible vs grader-visible state) -> composed from pm-cxr0jb (intent-scoped read projections): the task projection is an intent-scoped read of a real item, and the grader reads the full item through the SDK. No new primitive needed; the boundary is two declared intents over one record.\n\n4. Bounded score projection -> pm-t5dt4z (self-reported token accounting) for cost-shaped rewards and pm-e96opw predicates for correctness-shaped rewards; the score is a declared projection over history (correctness, cost, route) rather than a new store.\n\nWhy the philosophy holds here: project management = context management, and an RL episode is a context problem twice over — the agent gets the smallest sufficient task description (a bounded projection, not the tracker), and the reward comes from recorded evidence (immutable history), never from agent self-report. pm as the environment other systems are tested in: not pm-a8lnoh/pm-9c7h9x/pm-atfm (those test pm itself). Delivery vehicle per the roadmap: domain-pack kit pm-g608fc + beyond-PM exemplar pm-iiv1gl prove the pattern; the RL pack is the second exemplar, not a fork of the first."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T21:26:16.740Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "RL, evaluation, benchmark, and sim-to-RL platform model\n\nAn episode manifest references a versioned task item, deterministic seed, allowed observation projection, hidden grader projection, action contract, resource budget, termination rules, and verifier version. Workspace snapshot/restore and deterministic id/time providers create byte-reproducible episode starts without sharing mutable state between concurrent episodes.\n\nThe public SDK exposes reset, observe, act, checkpoint, terminate, verify, and trajectory-stream primitives. Actions are ordinary governed PM mutations, so the same immutable record supports debugging, offline evaluation, reward recomputation, counterfactual replay, and audit. Observation budgets use the context engine; hidden fields are enforced by policy at the SDK boundary to prevent reward leakage.\n\nVerdicts combine structured acceptance criteria, linked tests, invariant checks, measurements, and forbidden-side-effect checks. Reward is derived from recorded evidence with component scores and a stable version; self-reported completion contributes no reward. Passing, failing, wrong-reason, timeout, policy-violation, and infrastructure-unavailable outcomes are distinguishable.\n\nThe same substrate supports supervised evals, offline RL datasets, benchmark leaderboards, simulation sweeps, curriculum generation, adversarial task variants, and sim-to-real promotion. Reproducibility requires trajectory schema versions, environment/package digests, provenance, privacy controls, and deterministic replay from the public SDK alone."
    edited_at: "2026-07-29T21:28:04.100Z"
